    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Based on the data provided, it seems that both an issue with the route and possible physical layer issues could be contributing to the high jitter and large spikes you're experiencing.\n\n1. The high latencies at hop 2 and 3 could imply routing issues. This hop could potentially have a heavily loaded physical connection or routing logic issues. It could also be possible that these hops span across different physical paths in the routing topology.\n2. The packet loss and inconsistency at hop 2 and 3 might also be causing the jitter. This could indicate a problems with a typical physical network like cable, switch or port on the machine. However, the sporadic nature of the spikes hints towards a routing or more natural scaling issue rather than an outright physical disconnection. \n\nChanging one at a time, you can:\n\n* Investigate route changes/stability at route hop 2 and potentially 3.\n* Swap cable, porting, hubs/switches potentially involved with the issue, or look for external sources for creating large packet transfer volumes. Mini on/off/off switch would be good as a test.\n* Double check config at router side for any misconfigurations or emergent issues with the networking hardware.\n\nHope this helps you in troubleshooting the issue.",
